# API parity waivers.
#
# The build-time audit (scripts/parity.rs, hooked into build.rs) requires every
# documented method/object under docs/api/ to be reachable from every supported
# platform — JS (wasm), Python, Swift, Kotlin. Entries listed here are
# intentional, load-bearing exceptions; each must come with a written reason
# so future maintainers can tell deliberate divergence from accidental drift.
#
# Format: one entry per non-comment line.
#   <doc_path>:<platforms_present>:<reason>
#
#   - doc_path           workspace-relative path of the docs/api/*.md file
#                        whose canonical signature does not exist on every
#                        platform (e.g. docs/api/core/shader/fetch.md).
#   - platforms_present  comma-separated subset of: web,python,swift,kotlin
#   - reason             one-line explanation; colons are allowed.
#
# Lines starting with `#` are comments. Blank lines are ignored.

# ---- Aliases (hidden override → canonical) ----
#
# These declare which canonical doc a hidden override credits when its
# filename does not strip to the canonical via the simple `_<platform>`
# suffix rule. Required when a single canonical method is split into
# multiple uniffi-bound methods (e.g. uniffi cannot marshal `&impl Trait`,
# so `Renderer::render` becomes one concrete method per (renderable × target)
# combination).

docs/api/core/renderer/hidden/render_mobile.md=>docs/api/core/renderer/render.md: uniffi cannot marshal &impl Renderable/Target; the single mobile render entry takes RenderableHandle + TargetHandle uniffi::Enums and Swift/Kotlin extensions overload the natural shapes back on top.

# Shader::new accepts both `String` and `Vec<String>` via `impl Into<ShaderInput>`.
# Uniffi cannot marshal `impl Into`, so mobile splits this into Shader(source)
# and the raw Shader.compose(parts) constructor. The Swift and Kotlin extensions
# spell the array form Shader([parts]). Both forms credit the canonical Shader::new docs.
docs/api/core/shader/hidden/compose_mobile.md=>docs/api/core/shader/new.md: Mobile splits Shader::new's impl-Into-based overload into a separate Shader.compose constructor; Swift/Kotlin extensions spell it Shader([parts]).

# Scene::load accepts a path or bytes via `impl Into<SceneSource>`. Mobile keeps
# Scene.load(path) and adds Scene.loadBytes(bytes); both credit the Scene::load docs.
docs/api/scene/scene/hidden/load_bytes_mobile.md=>docs/api/scene/scene/load.md: Mobile splits Scene::load's impl-Into-based overload into a separate Scene.loadBytes constructor.

# Shader::set_registry is a static method returning nothing. uniffi has no static
# methods, so mobile sets the registry through a useRegistry constructor and the
# Swift/Kotlin extensions spell it Shader.setRegistry(baseUrl).
docs/api/core/shader/hidden/use_registry_mobile.md=>docs/api/core/shader/set_registry.md: Mobile sets the registry through a useRegistry constructor; Swift/Kotlin extensions expose Shader.setRegistry with no return value.

# Shader::fetch is async, and uniffi 0.32 can't make a primary constructor async,
# so both the single-part (fetch_mobile) and multi-part (fetch_compose_mobile)
# bindings are async instance methods. The Swift and Kotlin extensions spell
# both as the static Shader.fetch(...).
docs/api/core/shader/hidden/fetch_mobile.md=>docs/api/core/shader/fetch.md: Mobile exposes fetch as an async instance method (uniffi 0.32 has no async primary constructor); Swift/Kotlin extensions spell it Shader.fetch(input).
docs/api/core/shader/hidden/fetch_compose_mobile.md=>docs/api/core/shader/fetch.md: Mobile multi-part fetch variant, the raw fetchCompose method; Swift/Kotlin extensions spell it Shader.fetch(parts).

# Pass::set_depth_target accepts a TextureTarget or a depth Texture via
# `impl TryInto<DepthTarget>`. Mobile takes the TextureTarget through
# setDepthTarget(TargetHandle) and the Texture through setDepthTexture;
# Swift/Kotlin extensions spell both as setDepthTarget(...).
docs/api/core/pass/hidden/set_depth_texture_mobile.md=>docs/api/core/pass/set_depth_target.md: Mobile splits the Texture form of Pass::set_depth_target into a separate setDepthTexture method.

# Pass::set_target takes a TextureTarget or a Texture in the same way. Mobile takes the
# TextureTarget through setTarget(TargetHandle) and the Texture through setTargetTexture;
# Swift/Kotlin extensions spell both as setTarget(...).
docs/api/core/pass/hidden/set_target_texture_mobile.md=>docs/api/core/pass/set_target.md: Mobile splits the Texture form of Pass::set_target into a separate setTargetTexture method.

# Material's five texture setters take impl Into<TextureInput>: a Texture, or bytes, a
# path, a URL or a mipmap chain that upload when the scene loads. Mobile takes the
# Texture through <slot>Texture(texture) and the other inputs through <slot>Source(input);
# Swift/Kotlin extensions spell both as <slot>Texture(...).
docs/api/scene/material/hidden/base_color_source_mobile.md=>docs/api/scene/material/base_color_texture.md: Mobile splits the texture input form of Material::base_color_texture into a separate baseColorSource method.
docs/api/scene/material/hidden/metallic_roughness_source_mobile.md=>docs/api/scene/material/metallic_roughness_texture.md: Mobile splits the texture input form of Material::metallic_roughness_texture into a separate metallicRoughnessSource method.
docs/api/scene/material/hidden/normal_source_mobile.md=>docs/api/scene/material/normal_texture.md: Mobile splits the texture input form of Material::normal_texture into a separate normalSource method.
docs/api/scene/material/hidden/occlusion_source_mobile.md=>docs/api/scene/material/occlusion_texture.md: Mobile splits the texture input form of Material::occlusion_texture into a separate occlusionSource method.
docs/api/scene/material/hidden/emissive_source_mobile.md=>docs/api/scene/material/emissive_texture.md: Mobile splits the texture input form of Material::emissive_texture into a separate emissiveSource method.

# ---- Types documented through their methods (no page of their own) ----
#
# `AlphaMode` exists in every binding. Its three values are documented on
# docs/api/scene/material/alpha_mode.md, the one method that takes it, like the
# other value enums (`TextureFormat`, `CompareFunction`). `TextureSlot` has a
# page of its own, docs/api/scene/texture_slot/texture_slot.md, since two
# methods take it.
#
# `LightKind` is what Rust's `Light::kind` returns. JS and Python also export
# the enum, Swift and Kotlin don't, and `Light.kind()` returns the kind's name
# as a string ("directional", "point", "spot") in every binding. The values
# are documented on docs/api/scene/light/kind.md. Returning `LightKind` from
# every binding is a breaking change, so it waits for 0.13.

# ---- Old type names kept as deprecated aliases ----
#
# Each binding's window and texture targets and `TextureRegion` carry the
# Rust name. The old names stay as aliases so existing code keeps working,
# and no doc shows them:
#   JS:     CanvasTarget -> WindowTarget
#   Python: RenderCanvasTarget -> WindowTarget
#   Swift/Kotlin: MobileWindowTarget -> WindowTarget, MobileTextureTarget -> TextureTarget,
#           TextureRegionMobile -> TextureRegion

# ---- Trait-level docs (no platform binding required) ----
#
# `Target` is a sealed Rust trait. uniffi 0.32 can export a trait and lets Swift
# and Kotlin implement one, but wasm_bindgen and pyo3 can't, and the crate-private
# `FrameSource` supertrait keeps every implementation inside the crate anyway.
# Its methods exist on every concrete target type (WindowTarget and TextureTarget
# in every binding), so the concrete
# `targets/window_target/*.md` and `targets/texture_target/*.md` entries enforce
# parity. These trait docs are kept for human reference and need no bindings
# of their own.
docs/api/targets/target/size.md::Trait method; parity enforced via concrete WindowTarget/TextureTarget docs.
docs/api/targets/target/resize.md::Trait method; parity enforced via concrete WindowTarget/TextureTarget docs.
docs/api/targets/target/get_image.md::Trait method; parity enforced via concrete WindowTarget/TextureTarget docs.

# ---- Hidden / not-yet-exposed conceptual docs ----
#
# `docs/api/web/external_texture.md` was moved to `docs/api/web/hidden/`
# because every implementation is still a stub returning
# RendererError::Error("not implemented yet"). See ROADMAP.md 0.11.1 entry —
# at least one concrete working example (likely web's HTMLVideoElement path)
# is required before the public-facing doc returns. Tracked across all
# platforms in the 0.13.x "Live media" milestone.

# Python has no portable native video-frame source equivalent to
# HTMLVideoElement / CVPixelBuffer / SurfaceTexture. Callers decode video
# frames host-side and upload via Texture.write() / Texture.write_region()
# instead. See docs/api/core/renderer/hidden/create_external_texture_py.md.
docs/api/core/renderer/create_external_texture.md:web,swift,kotlin:Python lacks a portable native video-frame source; use Texture.write/write_region to upload decoded frames instead.

# ---- Type names (binding-only classes) ----
#
# scripts/binding_checks.rs fails the build when a binding declares a class that
# the Rust crate root does not export in a plain Rust build. It reads the generated
# Swift wrapper (Kotlin gets the same uniffi names), the Python stub and the wasm
# exports. Each class below is an exception with its reason:
#   type <ClassName>:<platforms>:<reason>
# A class from the uniffi wrapper lists swift and kotlin. An entry for a class no
# binding declares, or for a Rust root export, fails the build too.

type RenderableHandle:swift,kotlin:uniffi can't marshal &impl Renderable, so mobile render and load take this enum; the Swift and Kotlin extensions overload the natural shapes on top.
type TargetHandle:swift,kotlin:uniffi can't marshal &impl Target, so mobile render and the pass target setters take this enum; the Swift and Kotlin extensions overload the natural shapes on top.
type SceneObjectHandle:swift,kotlin:uniffi can't marshal &impl SceneObject, so mobile Scene.add and Pass.add take this enum; the Swift and Kotlin extensions overload Model, Camera and Light on top.
type PassTarget:swift,kotlin:uniffi can't marshal impl Into<PassRef>, so the mobile Scene methods that pick a pass take this index-or-name enum.
type TextureInputMobile:swift,kotlin:uniffi can't marshal impl Into<TextureInput>, so the mobile texture inputs take this enum, which mirrors TextureData without Empty.
type UniformValue:swift,kotlin:uniffi can't marshal impl Into<UniformData>, so mobile Shader.set takes this enum; the Swift and Kotlin extensions overload set on top.
type UniformData:swift,kotlin:mobile Shader.get returns the uniform value itself; Rust keeps UniformData crate-private and reads values through Shader::get<T>.
type ArrayElement:swift,kotlin:part of UniformData, which mobile Shader.get returns and Rust keeps crate-private.
type PushEntry:swift,kotlin:part of UniformData, which mobile Shader.get returns and Rust keeps crate-private.
type StorageAccess:swift,kotlin:part of UniformData, which mobile Shader.get returns and Rust keeps crate-private.
type StorageEntry:swift,kotlin:part of UniformData, which mobile Shader.get returns and Rust keeps crate-private.
type StructField:swift,kotlin:part of UniformData, which mobile Shader.get returns and Rust keeps crate-private.
type StructShape:swift,kotlin:part of UniformData, which mobile Shader.get returns and Rust keeps crate-private.
type ExternalTextureHandle:web,swift,kotlin:Renderer.createExternalTexture returns this stub on the platforms with a native video frame source; Rust has no external texture API yet.
type RenderCanvasFrame:python:the frame object of the rendercanvas present protocol, which only Python uses.
type InvalidInputError:python:Python raises a FragmentColorError subclass for a value the call can't take, such as an int past u32, a bad size, or texture bytes that don't match their size; Rust reports a TextureError, SizeError or ScreenRegionError, and JavaScript an Error.
type UnsupportedMipmapFormatError:python:Python raises a FragmentColorError subclass for a format the CPU mipmap builder can't fill; Rust reports a TextureError.
type MalformedImageError:python:Python raises a FragmentColorError subclass for image bytes that can't be decoded; Rust reports a TextureError.
type OccludedSurfaceError:python:Python raises a FragmentColorError subclass for a hidden or minimized window; Rust reports SurfaceError::Occluded.
type DeviceError:python:Python raises a FragmentColorError subclass with kind and label attributes for a GPU error an error scope caught; Rust reports RendererError::DeviceError or TextureError::DeviceError, JavaScript an Error with kind and label properties, Swift FragmentColorError.Device and Kotlin FragmentColorException.Device.
